00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:08.940
And then the two of us sat there and did our impressions of the noises of things going

190
00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:09.980
in the trash can, like,

191
00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:19.740
I don't know.

192
00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.520
He's just so fun.

193
00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.500
I just adored him.

194
00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.660
That fast fact was just chock full of fun and fast facts.

195
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.600
That was a very full fast fact.

196
00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.280
It was a full fast fact.

197
00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.980
It was a full, fun, double fast fact.

198
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.840
Fantastically fun, full fast fact.

199
00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:38.460
Oh, my gosh.

200
00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.040
Fast fact number two.

201
00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:41.120
Okay.

202
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.860
Fast fact number two is a fan question from Zach Akers, and here's what he wrote in to

203
00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:47.220
say.

204
00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:54.840
Why does Jim do his talking heads in front of the outside facing window, while everyone

205
00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:01.020
else's talking heads are inside of the window that faces the bullpen of the office?

206
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.240
He says, in Boo's Cruise and some later episodes, there are joint Jim-Pam talking heads in front

207
00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:13.080
of the outside window, but neither Pam nor anyone else normally sits in front of that outside

208
00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:13.500
window.

209
00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:18.760
And then he noticed, because Jim has several talking heads in this one, he was like, it's

210
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.240
really prominent in this episode that Jim is there.

211
00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:28.880
So I thought that was a great question and such a wise observation I had to investigate.

212
00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.640
No, that is a great catch.

213
00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:32.660
It really is.

214
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:38.040
So I emailed Greg Daniels, and I asked him, was this deliberate?

215
00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.360
Was this an accident?

216
00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.360
What does he have to say?

217
00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:47.160
And he said that it was something that Randall Einhorn, our cinematographer, suggested to

218
00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.920
Greg, starting with the pilot, and Greg absolutely loved it.

219
00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:58.960
He said Randall's intention here was that it didn't necessarily have to be only Jim, but

220
00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:06.180
the idea was that any character that had a future outside of Dunder Mifflin or held some sort

221
00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.760
of internal optimism would sit in front of a window leading out into the world.

222
00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:18.160
But characters who didn't want to leave Dunder Mifflin or who maybe were in more of a position

223
00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.480
of being trapped would do their talking heads facing into the office.

224
00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:24.140
Wow.

225
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:25.280
That's so deep.

226
00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.320
That's really deep.

227
00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:27.980
Yeah.

228
00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:34.080
I'm sitting here racking my brain to think of my character ever sat on that other wall with

229
00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.240
the window facing out behind her, and the one that comes to my mind, and I think it might

230
00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.120
be the only one.

231
00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.880
I'm trying to think if there's any other one.

232
00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.600
It was when you and I did Big Pregs, Little Pregs.

233
00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:46.800
Yeah, we did.

234
00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:50.140
I think it's the only time I ever sat there.

235
00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:58.580
Well, for a very long time, I only sat there if I was with Jim, which was really the only

236
00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:01.360
possible future for Pam outside of the office.

237
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:02.920
Wow.

238
00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.540
I think, you know, isn't that crazy?

239
00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:11.220
But otherwise, she was rather trapped in her, you know, bad decision of being with Roy.

240
00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.240
Trapped or content, you know?

241
00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.520
I mean, some of us were just happy with our lot in life.

242
00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:17.020
Yes.

243
00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.740
Well, that is fascinating, and I like it.

244
00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:21.400
Yeah.

245
00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:22.600
I thought that was a good one.

246
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:26.480
So thank you so much, Zach, for pointing that out and writing in.

247
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:32.400
Now, finally, fast fact number three, Angela, it's a fast fact flashback.

248
00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:33.420
What are you doing?

249
00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:34.240
What are you up?

250
00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:34.960
